        ":question/id": "efe9bc87-3892-5729-86c2-f5e953c42646",
        ":question/math-academy-id": "q-43293",
        ":question/problem": "$\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }({sin}^{3}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )=$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "First, we expand the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }({sin}^{3}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\cdot {sin}^{3}⁡\\theta +\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\cdot {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{{sin}^{3}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }+\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\cancel{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }}{\\cancel{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }}+\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\cancel{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }}{\\cancel{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }} \\\\  & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that, in the last step, we simplified the right-hand side using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1.$ \n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }({sin}^{3}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )=1.\n$$"
      },
