        ":question/math-academy-id": "q-65222",
        ":question/problem": "Simplify the trigonometric expression $\\operatorname{cos}⁡t(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)-{sin}^{2}⁡t.$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "First, we expand the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)-{sin}^{2}⁡t & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-{cos}^{2}⁡t-{sin}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-({cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that, in the last step, we simplified the right-hand side using ${cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t=1.$ \n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡t(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)-{sin}^{2}⁡t=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-1.\n$$"
      },
